
Step 1 — Open the Sysinternals page
Go to the official Microsoft Sysinternals download page:
https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sysinternals/downloads/sysmon

Step 2 — Download the ZIP file
Click the Download Sysmon link.

Step 3 — Extract the ZIP
* Right-click → Extract All
or
WinRAR or 7-Zip
** Free, open-source, and very powerful
** High compression ratio using 7z format
** Supports ZIP, RAR (extract), TAR, etc.
** Uses strong AES-256 encryption

Or use PowerShell:
Expand-Archive -Path C:\Temp\Sysmon\Sysmon.zip -DestinationPath C:\Temp\Sysmon

After extraction, you’ll see:
Sysmon.exe → 32-bit version
Sysmon64.exe → 64-bit version (use this on modern lab machines)

Step 4 — Install Sysmon
Open PowerShell as Administrator
Navigate to the folder:
cd C:\Temp\Sysmon
Install Sysmon as a service with default settings:
.\Sysmon64.exe -accepteula -i
-i → install
-accepteula → accept license automatically

Step 3 — Download a Sysmon Config File
Using a config file reduces noise and captures the events you care about. We’ll use SwiftOnSecurity’s baseline.
# Download Sysmon configuration
Invoke-WebRequest -Uri https://raw.githubusercontent.com/SwiftOnSecurity/sysmon-config/master/sysmonconfig-export.xml -OutFile C:\Temp\Sysmon\sysmonconfig.xml

Step 4 — Apply the Config File
.\Sysmon64.exe -c C:\Temp\Sysmon\sysmonconfig.xml
-c → apply or update configuration
You can rerun this whenever you want to update rules without reinstalling Sysmon.

Step 5 — Verify Sysmon Installation
In PowerShell:

sysmon -c  # Shows current config
sysmon -s  # Shows status of the service

Open Event Viewer:

Applications and Services Logs → Microsoft → Windows → Sysmon → Operational
You should start seeing events like:
Event ID 1 → Process creation
Event ID 3 → Network connections

Step 6 — Integrate with Wazuh Agent
Open your Wazuh agent configuration file:
notepad "C:\Program Files (x86)\ossec-agent\ossec.conf"
Add a block to collect Sysmon logs: ("Before </ossec_config>" and "END of Default Configuration" at the bottom of the file).

<localfile>
  <log_format>eventchannel</log_format>
  <location>Microsoft-Windows-Sysmon/Operational</location>
</localfile>

Save the file and restart Wazuh agent:
net stop wazuh-agent
net start wazuh-agent

Step 7 — Test Sysmon Logging
Open PowerShell and run:
notepad.exe
powershell -c "whoami"

Check Event Viewer → Sysmon logs → you should see Event ID 1 for each process creation.
Check Wazuh dashboard → after a few minutes, these events should appear.

Step 8 — Optional: Update Sysmon Config Later
If you want to refine detection:
# Reapply updated config
.\Sysmon64.exe -c C:\Temp\Sysmon\sysmonconfig.xml
